content:
Developers love building Telegram bots using C# and the popular Telegram.Bot library. It is powerful, fast, and well-maintained. However, as soon as your bot grows beyond a simple “Hello World” echo bot, you run into a classic architectural problem: The Update Handler Spaghetti.
If you have ever built a conversational bot, your main update handler probably looks something like this:
async Task HandleUpdateAsync(ITelegramBotClient botClient, Update update, CancellationToken ct)
{
if (update.Message is { } message)
{
switch (message.Text)
{
case "/start":
// Send welcome message
break;
default:
if (userState == UserState.AwaitingName)
{
// Save name, ask for age, change state...
}
else if (userState == UserState.AwaitingAge)
{
// Save age, finish registration...
}
break;
}
}
}As you add more features, states, and callback queries, this single method quickly turns into an unmaintainable nightmare.
To solve this issue and bring Clean Architecture principles to Telegram bot development, we can organize our code using controllers and a Finite State Machine (FSM).

How a Clean Bot Project Should Look
Instead of dumping all routing, state checks, and business logic into one or two files, you can organize your codebase into logical, highly maintainable folders:
MyTelegramBot/
├── Controllers/
│ ├── MainController.cs
│ └── RegistrationController.cs
├── States/
│ └── UserState.cs
├── Program.cs
└── MyTelegramBot.csprojhis structure is made possible by Telegram.Bot.Mediator.

Getting Started
Let’s build a simple multi-step registration bot (asking for a name and then an age) using Telegram.Bot.Mediator.
1. Install the Package
Add the package to your project. It relies on standard Microsoft extensions and the Telegram.Bot library:
dotnet add package Telegram.Bot.Mediator --version 1.0.152. Define Your States
First, define the states of your conversation flow inside States/UserState.cs:
public enum UserState
{
Default,
AwaitingName,
AwaitingAge
}3. Create Your Controllers
Now we split the logic into controllers. Let’s create Controllers/RegistrationController.cs inheriting from BotController:
using Telegram.Bot;
using Telegram.Bot.Types;
using Telegram.Bot.Mediator;public class RegistrationController : BotController
{
private readonly ITelegramBotClient _botClient;
public RegistrationController(ITelegramBotClient botClient)
{
_botClient = botClient;
}
// Handles the "/start" command when the user is in the Default state
[MessageRoute("/start")]
public async Task StartRegistration(Message message)
{
await _botClient.SendTextMessageAsync(message.Chat.Id, "Welcome! Please enter your name:");
// Transition user to the next state
await UpdateUserStateAsync(message.Chat.Id, UserState.AwaitingName);
}
// Handles text messages only when the user is in the AwaitingName state
[StateRoute(UserState.AwaitingName)]
public async Task HandleName(Message message)
{
string userName = message.Text;
await _botClient.SendTextMessageAsync(message.Chat.Id, $"Nice to meet you, {userName}! Now, how old are you?");
// Move to the next state
await UpdateUserStateAsync(message.Chat.Id, UserState.AwaitingAge);
}
// Handles text messages only when the user is in the AwaitingAge state
[StateRoute(UserState.AwaitingAge)]
public async Task HandleAge(Message message)
{
if (int.TryParse(message.Text, out int age))
{
await _botClient.SendTextMessageAsync(message.Chat.Id, $"Thank you! Registration complete with age: {age}.");
// Reset state back to Default
await UpdateUserStateAsync(message.Chat.Id, UserState.Default);
}
else
{
await _botClient.SendTextMessageAsync(message.Chat.Id, "Please enter a valid number.");
}
}
}
4. Configure Dependency Injection
In your Program.cs, register the mediator and your controllers. Because the package uses standard .Extensions abstractions, it fits perfectly into your dependency injection container:
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Telegram.Bot;
using Telegram.Bot.Mediator;
using Telegram.Bot.Mediator.Services;var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);
// 1. Register the standard Telegram Bot Client
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ITelegramBotClient>(new TelegramBotClient("YOUR_BOT_TOKEN"));
// 2. Register Telegram.Bot.Mediator with your UserState
builder.Services.AddTelegramBotMediator<UserState>(options =>
{
options.AddControllersFromAssembly(typeof(Program).Assembly);
});
// 3. Register the built-in background service for automatic polling
builder.Services.AddHostedService<BotBackgroundService<UserState>>();
var host = builder.Build();
await host.RunAsync();
And that is literally it!
With about 15 lines of code in Program.cs, you have a fully functioning, state-routed, controller-based Telegram bot running as a standard .NET background service.
